//! The help bodies of the status commands: `track`, `ignore` and `finish`
//! (da-udkd3), out of `commands.rs` for its size.

pub(super) const TRACK_BODY: &str = "USAGE:\n  datum track <FIELD>[,<FIELD>...] [SETUP CHANGES] [-y / -q / -d / -t / -z]\n\n`track F …` is `datum setup F … --track`: it takes every flag `setup`\ntakes (`datum help setup`), and the time flags of a log. The spec's changes and\nthe row saying tracking began are one write, and one `undo`.\n\n  datum track vacuum --kind moment -k next_date=+4 --assign proj=household\n\nA list, `track coffee,tea,mood`, is a `track` of each in that order, each\nechoed as alone and each with the same changes. A name refused alone is\nrefused in a list, and then nothing of it is written.\n\nOn a field that has a spec, `track F` alone changes nothing of it: it\nwrites the row and nothing else. On a field with none, it declares one,\nas `setup F --kind K --track` does; `--kind` may be left out when the\nfield's log answers it, and a new field is asked what a press of it\nmeans.\n\nTracking is not what puts a field in `datum now` — every field that has\never been logged is already there (`datum now -A` shows the whole set).\nWhat the spec does is give a field its own words. `track mood --kind\nchange --add-state good` makes `datum mood` mean `change mood` and `datum\ngood` mean `change mood good`; `datum alias list` prints them. That spec\nis itself an entry, `spec:mood`, so it syncs and backs up with the rows.\n\nThe row is written at now, or where the time flags place it (`track\ncoffee -t 09:00`, `-y`, `-d 2026-10-01 -t 8`, `datum help time`), and the\nspec, which has no time, is the same either way. It carries the TRACKED\nstate, whatever the field's kind: tracked, and in no state yet. `tail`\ndraws it in gray, as the marker of when tracking began. It is what un-ignores an ignored\nfield, as any later log does — see `datum help ignore`. A field that\nalready has entries and is not ignored gets no row, so a RUNNING field\nkeeps running, and `track F` alone on it says «no change».\n\nThe row is echoed as any log is, its action `TRACK` and the glyph of the\nfield's kind (`TRACK●`, `TRACK▬`, `TRACK▨`, `TRACK¢`).\n";

pub(super) const IGNORE_BODY: &str = "USAGE:\n  datum ignore <FIELD>[,<FIELD>...] [SETUP CHANGES] [-y / -q / -d / -t / -z]\n\n`ignore F …` is `datum setup F … --ignore`, as `track` is `setup … --track`\n(`datum help track`): `setup`'s flags, one write and one `undo`. `ignore F`\nalone changes no spec: it writes the row, and on a field with no spec it\ndeclares none.\n\nA list, `ignore coffee,tea`, is an `ignore` of each in that order, each\nechoed as alone; a name refused alone refuses the list, and nothing of it\nis written. A field name has no comma, so `a,b` is never one field.\n\nIgnoring is the other half of `datum track`, and it is one row: a change\nentry carrying the IGNORED sentinel. A field is ignored\nwhen its NEWEST state-bearing row is that sentinel — ignored-ness is\nread off the log, never off a flag — so the next `moment`/`on`/\n`change` on the field un-ignores it, as `datum track` does. A field\nignored already gets no second row: «no change». The row is echoed as any\nlog is, its action `IGNORE`.\n\nWhat that hides is the field's place in the views that ask what exists:\n`datum now` drops it from the list (`now -A` shows it again, dimmed,\nbelow a rule), `datum fields` stops listing it, and `datum info` counts\nit under `ignored` instead of `tracked`. Nothing else moves — the\nentries stay, and `tail` / `get` / `grep` still find them.\n\nThe row lands at now, or where the time flags place it, as `track`'s:\n`ignore coffee -y` ignores it from this time yesterday. It needs no\nfield to ignore: naming one that was never logged INVENTS it, with the\nsentinel as the only row it has.\n";

pub(super) const FINISH_BODY: &str = "USAGE:\n  datum finish <FIELD>[,<FIELD>...] [-y / -q / -d / -t / -z]\n\nFinishing a field says it is done for good: an experiment ended, an issue\nclosed. It is one row, a change entry carrying the FINISHED sentinel, at\nnow or where the time flags place it, echoed with the action `FINISH`. A\nfield finished already gets no second row: «no change».\n\nIt is terminal. A new entry dated after it is refused, naming the FINISH\nentry. One dated before it is written as any is, and so is one with no time\n(a remark, a meta note), and the field's spec still changes. The way back\nfrom a finish made by mistake is to change that entry's state, `datum\nupdate <HID> state=false` (or IGNORED), after which entries are logged again.\n\nA finished field is hidden as an ignored one is: `datum now` drops it\n(`now -A` shows it), `datum fields` and datum.day's collect tab leave it out,\nand `datum info` counts it under `ignored`. Its entries stay, and `tail`\nand `dayview` still show its past.\n\nA copy that syncs takes every entry as it comes, after a finish or not;\n`datum check integrity` names each entry dated after its field's FINISHED.\n";
